  "text": "If you put $100 on a negative 500 odds bet for 30 days straight, you will lose the hold. Respect this creator. Respect anybody making content on here, but I wanted to point out the math on this trend because I'm actually starting to see it quite a lot. And ultimately, the math of this trend will just have you losing the hold over that 30 day challenge. And just like any of these betting trends, ultimately, if you are not betting with an edge, you are always just going to regress to whatever the hold you're betting into. I want to walk through the example that he is betting today, and I want to show you why the hold is not your friend in this situation. All right, so he is taking an alternate royal runs. He got a bit of a different odds, but this is what it currently is. Why I want to know both of these informations is using both of these odds, we can calculate how much hold is baked into this bet. First, in order to do this, we got to convert the odds to implied probability. So minus 500, this is an 83.3% implied probability, and plus 370 is an implied probability of 21.27%. Now, to get the hold of this market, all we have to do is add those two probabilities together and anything greater than one shows us our hold. So in this case, we're betting into a market with a 4.601% hold. Remember, the house, the hold is the house's edge. This is how much that you have to overcome in order to break even. This is how much they're expected to make if you keep betting this over and over and over. Now, the reason I want to point this out is when we look at alternative lines, they are very much informed by what the current market is showing us on the over under or the spread. So if we were to just look at the main market of the game, we can do the same thing with these two numbers. We can look at how much hold is baked within this market. 118 or minus 118 has an implied probability of 54.13% break even, and minus 102 is 50.5%. Now we can sum those and we can figure out what the hold is. In this case, the hold of this market is 4.62%, so it's actually a little bit higher than the alternative spread. Remember, the alternative spread was 4.61%. So it's 0.1% lower. But ultimately what this means is there's no difference in your advantage from betting the alternative line or the main line. So when you're guessing and you're just deciding, okay, I want to, I'm going to convert the odds to something much, much more probable of happening. You're not doing what you think you're accomplishing because the amount of hold you're betting into doesn't change. You're betting into the same disadvantage, about 4.6%. So the more bets you make every day, the more you're just going to regress to that hold. There's no difference here than just betting and guessing on the main market line. The only difference is because you're converting your implied probability to a much more probable break even, remember he's instead of betting 102 or 118, he's betting minus 500, which will win 80 ish percent of the time. He's just, he's just kicking the can to a later bet in the week when he will ultimately lose, but he will ultimately lose about the same amount. If he just bet this every day or over the next 30 days, he bets the minus 500. He's going to regress to losing about 4.6% of his money. Look, keep grinding. I get it. A lot of sports betting is entertainment is, you know, some version of trying to create lottery tickets and, you know, have a big payoff, but ultimately be cautious of trends like this because ultimately if you're not doing anything to overcome the hold with some type of advantage, you're just going to regress to the hold over the longterm. And remember you have a negative advantage. The house has the edge against you unless otherwise stated or overcome. I hope this helped guys. I commend anybody making content on here. So in no way is this like a slight to this. I just thought it was a really educational moment and opportunity because I don't think anybody's kind of looking at the math behind this and why ultimately it isn't the kind of ticket lottery ticket system that maybe people are thinking it is. If you have any questions, let us know and as always, good luck.",
